edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525963 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186525572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525963 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186525617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525963 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186525668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525963 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186525691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525963 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525963 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525963 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186525668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525963 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e996ac02-f7f8-52a5-bf48-73bf10f4b3be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525991 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186525849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525991 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345598" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525991 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525991 #edb/ref 1071 "A water pollution researcher measured $4$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},$ for each water sample in a region with suspicions of water pollution. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular sample.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=9,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=3,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=1.\n$$\n\nThe researcher then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{1},$ where ${V}_{1}$ is the $4\\times 1$ matrix consisting of the first principal component of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525991 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1b0e0b9e-9780-58a6-9804-62e273336ea3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525991 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{4}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2},{\\sigma }_{4}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(9)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2} \\\\  & =95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  researcher used only the first principal component, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(9)^{2} \\\\  & =81.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{81}{95} \\\\  & ≈0.853 \\\\  & =85.3%.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526003 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186525963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526003 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345569" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526003 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526003 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}| &  & | \\\\ {u}_{1} & ⋯ & {u}_{n} \\\\ | &  & |\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}| &  & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & ⋯ & {v}_{m} \\\\ | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be a $n\\times m$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the fourth principal component of the data?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526003 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "30651707-ffb3-536f-b51e-a5a6fc797509" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526003 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ Notice that:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ$ is a diagonal $m\\times m$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$, and\n\n- $V$ is a $m\\times m$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the fourth principal component is ${v}_{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526020 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186525862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526020 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345600" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526020 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526020 #edb/ref 1071 "A quality inspector measured $5$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ for each machine in a factory. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular machine.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=6,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=4,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=1,\\,{\\sigma }_{5}=0.5.\n$$\n\nThe inspector then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{2},$ where ${V}_{2}$ is the $5\\times 2$ matrix consisting of the first two principal components of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526020 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "544f3b17-0710-5a62-83f3-a61ed0f7bcdd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526020 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},…,{\\sigma }_{5}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(0.5)^{2} \\\\  & =57.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the inspector used only the first two principal components, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2}+(4)^{2} \\\\  & =52.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{52}{57.25} \\\\  & ≈0.908 \\\\  & =90.8%.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526041 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186525901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526041 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345566" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526041 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526041 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}| &  & | \\\\ {u}_{1} & ⋯ & {u}_{n} \\\\ | &  & |\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}| &  & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & ⋯ & {v}_{m} \\\\ | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be a $n\\times m$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the first principal component of the data?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526041 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7686a2d1-5498-5a0d-a8d1-3fd56d01887b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526041 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ Notice that:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ$ is a diagonal $m\\times m$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$, and\n\n- $V$ is a $m\\times m$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the first principal component is ${v}_{1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526045 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186525873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526045 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345605" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526045 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526045 #edb/ref 1071 "A nutritionist measured $5$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ for each client in a group under a specific diet. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular client.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=6,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=3,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=1,\\,{\\sigma }_{5}=0.6.\n$$\n\nThe nutritionist then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{2},$ where ${V}_{2}$ is the $5\\times 2$ matrix consisting of the first two principal components of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526045 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7dcc2367-ae8e-5075-82b4-bcd5d8410a2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526045 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},…,{\\sigma }_{5}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(0.6)^{2} \\\\  & =50.36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the nutritionist used only the first two principal components, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2}+(3)^{2} \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{45}{50.36} \\\\  & ≈0.894 \\\\  & =89.4%.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526056 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186525921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526056 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345556" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526056 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526056 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & 0 & -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & 0 & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be a $2\\times 3$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the third principal component of the data?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526056 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "94503ed0-de40-59e8-a833-79a2a877bd96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526056 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>{\\lambda }_{3}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- the $3$rd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ corresponding to the $3$rd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}.$\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ Notice that:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ$ is a diagonal $3\\times 3$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$, and\n\n- $V$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the third principal component is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526064 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186525960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526064 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345546" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526064 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526064 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be a $2\\times 3$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the first principal component of the data?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526064 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a56a93f9-03e9-5541-a7b2-0bb8dffd858c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526064 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>{\\lambda }_{3}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- the $3$rd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ corresponding to the $3$rd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}.$\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ Notice that:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ$ is a diagonal $3\\times 3$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$, and\n\n- $V$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the first principal component is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526092 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186525864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526092 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345607" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526092 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526092 #edb/ref 1071 "A pharmacologist measured $5$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ for each drug among a group of potential treatments for high blood pressure. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular drug.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=6,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=3,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=1.5,\\,{\\sigma }_{5}=1.\n$$\n\nThe pharmacologist then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{1},$ where ${V}_{1}$ is the $5\\times 1$ matrix consisting of the first principal component of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526092 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e91cdcf4-5450-5f10-aac1-2b321d212e78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526092 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},…,{\\sigma }_{5}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1.5)^{2}+(1)^{2} \\\\  & =52.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the researcher used only the first principal component, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2} \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{36}{52.25} \\\\  & ≈0.689 \\\\  & =68.9%.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
